Union-Find Problem :
===================
Given a disjointed set of non overlapping regions, given a new entry you find out which set it belongs to (Find problem).
In addition, you may want to to a Union of two disjointed sets.

I m suggesting using a TreeMap<Key, Disjoint_Set<T>> for this. 
Key tells you which Set you belong to. Its a sorted tree so we d have nlg(n) search/insert operations.

If both ends are in the same set then do nothing with this entry.

If one vertex exists the other does not, then add both vertices in the TreeMap and have them point to the same
Disjoint_Set.

If both vertices point to entries that exist and their Disjoint Sets are different, then do a Union of the 
two disjoint_sets.  This can be efficiently done by having each Set internally have an id. Union of two 
Sets means clobbering their IDs to have the same value. The keys in the TreeMap would continue pointing to 
the old Set pointers but these pointers would have values that are equivalent now.
